Task 1: Create OpenShift Routes to Expose Applications
Subtask 1.1: Deploy Sample Applications
First, let's create some sample applications that we can expose using routes.

Create a new project for this lab:
oc new-project route-lab
Deploy a simple web application:
oc new-app --name=webapp1 --docker-image=httpd:2.4 -l app=webapp1
Deploy a second web application:
oc new-app --name=webapp2 --docker-image=nginx:1.20 -l app=webapp2
Verify the deployments are running:
oc get pods
Wait until both pods show Running status before proceeding.

Subtask 1.2: Create Services for the Applications
Expose the first application as a service:
oc expose deployment webapp1 --port=8080 --target-port=80
Expose the second application as a service:
oc expose deployment webapp2 --port=8080 --target-port=80
Verify services are created:
oc get services
You should see both webapp1 and webapp2 services listed.

Subtask 1.3: Create Basic Routes
Create a route for the first application:
oc expose service webapp1
Create a route for the second application:
oc expose service webapp2
View the created routes:
oc get routes
Get detailed information about the routes:
oc describe route webapp1
oc describe route webapp2
Test external access to your applications:
# Get the route URL for webapp1
WEBAPP1_URL=$(oc get route webapp1 -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
echo "WebApp1 URL: http://$WEBAPP1_URL"

# Test the connection
curl -I http://$WEBAPP1_URL
Task 2: Set Up Path-Based Routing for Services
Subtask 2.1: Create a Custom Route with Path-Based Routing
Path-based routing allows you to serve multiple applications from a single hostname using different URL paths.

Create a YAML file for path-based routing:
cat > path-based-route.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: multi-app-route
  namespace: route-lab
spec:
  host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local
  path: /app1
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: webapp1
    weight: 100
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
---
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: multi-app-route-2
  namespace: route-lab
spec:
  host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local
  path: /app2
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: webapp2
    weight: 100
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
EOF
Apply the path-based routes:
oc apply -f path-based-route.yaml
Verify the path-based routes:
oc get routes
Subtask 2.2: Create a Main Landing Page Route
Deploy a simple landing page application:
oc new-app --name=landing --docker-image=httpd:2.4 -l app=landing
Expose the landing page service:
oc expose deployment landing --port=8080 --target-port=80
Create a route for the root path:
cat > landing-route.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: landing-route
  namespace: route-lab
spec:
  host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: landing
    weight: 100
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
EOF
Apply the landing page route:
oc apply -f landing-route.yaml
Subtask 2.3: Test Path-Based Routing
Test the different paths:
# Test the landing page (root path)
curl -H "Host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local" http://$(oc get route landing-route -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')

# Test app1 path
curl -H "Host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local" http://$(oc get route multi-app-route -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')/app1

# Test app2 path
curl -H "Host: multi-app.apps.cluster.local" http://$(oc get route multi-app-route-2 -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')/app2
Task 3: Implement Load Balancing for Exposed Services
Subtask 3.1: Scale Applications for Load Balancing
Scale the first application to multiple replicas:
oc scale deployment webapp1 --replicas=3
Scale the second application to multiple replicas:
oc scale deployment webapp2 --replicas=2
Verify the scaling:
oc get pods -l app=webapp1
oc get pods -l app=webapp2
Subtask 3.2: Configure Load Balancing Strategy
Create a route with specific load balancing configuration:
cat > load-balanced-route.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: load-balanced-webapp
  namespace: route-lab
  annotations:
    haproxy.router.openshift.io/balance: roundrobin
    haproxy.router.openshift.io/disable_cookies: "true"
spec:
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: webapp1
    weight: 100
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
EOF
Apply the load-balanced route:
oc apply -f load-balanced-route.yaml
View the route configuration:
oc describe route load-balanced-webapp
Subtask 3.3: Create Multi-Service Load Balancing
Create a route that balances between multiple services:
cat > multi-service-route.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: multi-service-balance
  namespace: route-lab
  annotations:
    haproxy.router.openshift.io/balance: roundrobin
spec:
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: webapp1
    weight: 70
  alternateBackends:
  - kind: Service
    name: webapp2
    weight: 30
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
EOF
Apply the multi-service route:
oc apply -f multi-service-route.yaml
Test the load balancing:
# Get the route URL
BALANCE_URL=$(oc get route multi-service-balance -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')

# Make multiple requests to see load balancing in action
for i in {1..10}; do
  echo "Request $i:"
  curl -s http://$BALANCE_URL | head -1
  sleep 1
done
Subtask 3.4: Monitor Load Balancing Performance
Check the router logs to see load balancing in action:
# Find the router pod
ROUTER_POD=$(oc get pods -n openshift-ingress -l ingresscontroller.operator.openshift.io/deployment-ingresscontroller=default -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.name}')

# View recent logs
oc logs -n openshift-ingress $ROUTER_POD --tail=50
Monitor service endpoints:
oc get endpoints webapp1 webapp2
Check route status and statistics:
oc get routes -o wide
Advanced Configuration and Troubleshooting
Secure Routes with TLS
Create a secure route with edge termination:
cat > secure-route.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: route.openshift.io/v1
kind: Route
metadata:
  name: secure-webapp
  namespace: route-lab
spec:
  to:
    kind: Service
    name: webapp1
    weight: 100
  port:
    targetPort: 8080
  tls:
    termination: edge
    insecureEdgeTerminationPolicy: Redirect
EOF
Apply the secure route:
oc apply -f secure-route.yaml
Test the secure route:
SECURE_URL=$(oc get route secure-webapp -o jsonpath='{.spec.host}')
curl -k https://$SECURE_URL
Common Troubleshooting Commands
Check route status:
oc get routes
oc describe route <route-name>
Verify service endpoints:
oc get endpoints
Check pod connectivity:
oc port-forward <pod-name> 8080:80
View router configuration:
oc get pods -n openshift-ingress
oc logs -n openshift-ingress <router-pod-name>
